.TH "UPTERM" "1" "Oct 2026" "Upterm 0.0.0+dev" "Upterm Manual"

.SH NAME
upterm-session-list - List running sessions


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBupterm session list [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
List the running sessions, one row each: name, status, session ID, command
and relay. * marks the session this terminal is in ($UPTERM_ADMIN_SOCKET).

.PP
The sessions come from the records in: /home/user/.local/state/upterm/results
Sessions started under another XDG_RUNTIME_DIR are listed too. Listing
deletes the records of sessions that ended more than 7 days ago, after which
\&'upterm session info' and 'upterm session wait' no longer know them.

.PP
-o json prints a JSON array of what 'upterm session info NAME -o json'
prints, one per running session, and [] when none is running.

.PP
Exit status: 0, or 1 when the session records can't be read.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for list

.PP
\fB--hide-client-ip\fP[=false]
	Hide guests' IP addresses from upterm's output. When it isn't set (by flag, environment or config file), they are hidden in CI only; --hide-client-ip=false shows them there too.

.PP
\fB-o\fP, \fB--output\fP=""
	Output format: json (command line only)


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--debug\fP[=false]
	Enable debug logging (log file: /home/user/.local/state/upterm/upterm.log). A non-empty DEBUG turns it on too.


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  # List the running sessions:
  upterm session list

  # As JSON, for a script:
  upterm session list -o json
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBupterm-session(1)\fP


